<title>GERMANY: BASF hikes dividend to 1.70 dmk, profit up.</title>
<headline>BASF hikes dividend to 1.70 dmk, profit up.</headline>
<dateline>LUDWIGSHAFEN, Germany 1997-03-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Calendar 1996</p>
<p>(in millions of marks unless stated)</p>
<p>    Group profit after tax		    2,790   vs  2,471</p>
<p>    Group pre-tax profit			4,414   vs  4,128</p>
<p>    Group income taxes			  1,575   vs  1,705</p>
<p>    Group sales				  48,776   vs 46,229</p>
<p>    Parent profit after tax		   1,701   vs  1,354</p>
<p>    Parent pre-tax profit		     2,215   vs  2,301</p>
<p>    Parent sales				 20,607   vs 21,061</p>
<p>    --sales in Germany			  6,304   vs  7,026</p>
<p>    --exports				    14,303   vs 14,035</p>
<p>    Dividend (proposed)			 1.70    vs 1.40 marks</p>
<p>    Dividend payout			     1,051   vs    854</p>
<p>    Transfer to reserves			  650   vs    500</p>
<p>NOTE - Full name of the German chemicals and drugs group is BASF AG. Analysts expected a five percent rise in pre-tax profits with estimates lying between 4.30 billion and 4.369 billion marks. Sales were expected to climb about 3.5 percent to somewhere between 47.6 billion and 48.2 billion marks and. And the dividend was expected to rise by 20 pfennigs to 1.60 marks. BASF will detail its results at a news conference on April 2.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
</text>
